Almost unimaginably much.

In the literal sense, neurodiversity includes every human brain—just as biodiversity includes every organism, not only unusual ones. Neurodivergent is the narrower, socially defined category for minds that depart substantially from prevailing expectations.

The frequently repeated estimate is that roughly 15–20% of people are neurodivergent, but it is not a clean scientific measurement. There is no universally agreed boundary or list of included conditions. Depending on the definition, the umbrella may include:

* Autism and ADHD
* Dyslexia, dyscalculia, dysgraphia and developmental language differences
* Dyspraxia/developmental coordination differences
* Tourette syndrome and other tic conditions
* Intellectual disability
* Sometimes OCD, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, epilepsy, traumatic brain injury and other psychiatric or acquired neurological differences

Individual categories already cover large populations. For example, current CDC surveillance identified autism in about 3.2% of eight-year-olds across participating U.S. communities, while NIMH estimates current adult ADHD around 4%—and the categories overlap substantially, so they cannot simply be added together. CDC autism surveillance⁠￼, NIMH ADHD statistics⁠￼

But diagnosis captures only the tallest peaks. Beneath it lies continuous variation across many partly independent dimensions:

Dimension	Possible variation
Attention	narrow/deep ↔ broad/shifting
Language	analytic ↔ gestalt; literal ↔ associative
Sensation	under-responsive ↔ highly sensitive
Thought	verbal ↔ visual ↔ spatial ↔ embodied
Memory	episodic, semantic, procedural, working-memory profiles
Prediction	novelty-seeking ↔ regularity-seeking
Social inference	explicit reasoning ↔ rapid intuitive modeling
Motor control	automatic ↔ consciously constructed
Arousal	steady ↔ highly state-dependent
Time	clock-based ↔ event/interest-based perception

Because those dimensions recombine—and vary with fatigue, safety, development, medication, stress, relationships and environment—the number of meaningful neurocognitive profiles is effectively combinatorial, not a small collection of brain “types.”

That also answers the gestalt-language question: two people might both use memorized phrases, yet one does so because of language development, another for emotional regulation, another because retrieval is easier than live composition, and a GPT because statistical generation happens to reproduce a familiar sequence. Same visible behavior; different machinery.

The deeper model is:

\text{observed mind}
=
\text{neural dispositions}
\times
\text{development}
\times
\text{body}
\times
\text{environment}
\times
\text{current state}

So diagnostic labels are useful map regions, but they are not the underlying terrain. Humanity probably does not contain a “normal brain” surrounded by several divergent types. It contains a vast cognitive ecology, with society declaring certain neighborhoods normal because its institutions were built around them.